# 🃏 Dealer's Choice

The theme for this game jam is dealer's choice. Build a complete card game of
your own design. You are responsible for naming the game and you may not use
"Dealer's Choice" as the name of your game.

Your game must meet these requirements:

- It must be a card game with custom-designed cards.
  You must design your own cards. You may not use the standard 52-card deck, nor
  any variation on a French deck of four suits and thirteen ranks.
- It must be original.
  Your game may not be a reskin of existing, well-known card games. Similarity
  to existing games will have a severe negative impact on your game's score.

At the end of the game jam, your submission will be judged against the other
contestants' submissions. Judges will be looking at how fun the game is to play,
playtime and replayability, and overall aesthetics and polish. Your goal is to
take first place in the jam.

## workspace

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Build the game there.

The workspace is a git repository, initialized before you started. Your finished
implementation is published directly from this repository's commits, and any
uncommitted work is **not** published.

Create any number of commits as you go. Once the game is complete, verify that
no unintended untracked files exist and that you have committed a README.md that
describes your game. Your README.md should be written with a prospective player
as the target audience.

## Your time budget

You have {{time_limit_hours}} hours of wall-clock time for this run. Running
`date` in the container reports the current time, so you can see how much of the
budget remains.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that
pins Playwright as a dev dependency, with its dependencies already installed.
`node_modules/` is populated and headless Chromium is downloaded, so you can
build and verify right away. Build on this project: keep the `package.json`, add
your framework, bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the
lockfile in sync.

There is no global Playwright on `PATH`. Use the project's own instance by
importing it (`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or by running `npx
playwright`. This environment has no Chromium sandbox, so Chromium has to be
launched with the `--no-sandbox` argument.

## Producing the game's assets

You must produce the art, animations, effects, and sound for your game. If you
use placeholder rectangles, stand-ins drawn at runtime, or omit assets, your
game will be judged extremely poorly when the game jam submissions are judged.

Six asset-generation binaries are on your `PATH` to help. Run each with `--help`
to learn its operations:

- `draw`, `draw-sheet`: 2D sprites and animated sprite sheets.
- `particle-2d`: 2D particle effects.
- `sfx-synth`, `sfx-sample`: sound effects, synthesized or sample-based.
- `music`: music tracks.

Use them, or produce assets any other way you prefer. These binaries are only
available during this run and not when your submission is published. Generate
any assets once and commit them. Do not define build commands that call into
the asset generation binaries.

## The fixed build/serve interface

The project stays a Node project built with only Node.js and npm-installed
dependencies. Extend the provided `package.json` and commit a
`package-lock.json`, running `npm install` after changing dependencies so the
lockfile matches. `npm ci` followed by `npm run build`, with no other manual
step, must produce the static site into one of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at
the project root, with an `index.html` at the root of that directory. Serving
that directory as-is must run the game.

The build must also work at **any base path**. It is played back from a per-run
sub-path, so every URL it constructs at runtime, including every produced asset,
must be page-relative and never start with `/`. For a bundler, set a relative
base such as `base: './'`.

You can write your game's core logic in Rust and compile it to WebAssembly if
you prefer: this container has the Rust toolchain, the `wasm32-unknown-unknown`
target, and `wasm-bindgen`/`wasm-pack` on `PATH`. As with the asset generation
tools, these are on `PATH` only while this run is live, so the compiled wasm is
a build input rather than a build step. Compile it once during the run, commit
the resulting `.wasm` and any generated JS glue, and have `npm run build` bundle
the committed output without invoking `cargo` or `wasm-pack`. Plain JavaScript
or TypeScript is acceptable too; reach for wasm only if it genuinely helps your
game.
